{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1554 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "026272fa1cc6bc95a5979782320d80892a26d9aaf188dfebab35041f8bd32b12" :edb/db-before-t 1553 :edb/db-after-t 1554 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534866 50 #inst "2026-10-08T09:31:59.384Z" 13194139534866 true] [17592186124171 1100 "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}.$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186124171 1100 "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,2)}} \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}$." 13194139534866 false] [17592186124171 1145 "The function\n\n$$\nf(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(-1,2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-1, 2))\\frac{xy^{3}}{x + y} &= ((-1) \\cdot 2^{3})/((-1) + 2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-8}{1} \\\\\n&=-8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534866 false] [17592186124171 1145 "The function \n\n$$\nf(x,y)= \\dfrac{xy^3}{x+y}\n$$\n\nis continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(-1,2)$ does not cause the denominator to become zero, so we can calculate the limit by substituting  $(-1,2)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,2)}\\frac{x{y}^{3}}{x+y} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot {2}^{3}}{(-1)+2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-8}{1} \\\\  & =-8\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186124172 1100 "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}.$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186124172 1100 "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2}$." 13194139534866 false] [17592186124172 1145 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 0 0\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2} = \\dfrac{(-1)^2(1) + (-1)(1)^2}{(-1)^2 - (1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-1, 1))\\frac{x^{2}y + xy^{2}}{x^{2} - y^{2}} &= lim_(x → (-1, 1))(xy(x + y))/((x + y)(x - y)) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (-1, 1))(xy)/((x - y))\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (-1, 1))(xy)/((x - y)) &= ((-1) \\cdot 1)/(-1 - 1) \\\\\n&= \\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$" 13194139534866 false] [17592186124172 1145 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac 0 0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (-1,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2y+xy^2}{x^2-y^2} = \\dfrac{(-1)^2(1) + (-1)(1)^2}{(-1)^2 - (1)^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}y+x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy(x+y)}{(x+y)(x-y)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1,1)}\\frac{xy}{(x-y)} & =\\frac{(-1)\\cdot 1}{-1-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $\\dfrac{1}{2}.$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186124176 1100 "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}.$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186124176 1100 "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2}$." 13194139534866 false] [17592186124176 1145 "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x$, we have\n$lim_(x → (0, 0)y = x)(x(x + 2y))/(y^{2})|= lim_(x → 0)(x(x + 2x))/(x^{2}); = lim_(x → 0)\\frac{3x^{2}}{x^{2}}; = 3$.\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x$, we have\n$lim_(x → (0, 0)y =-x)(x(x + 2y))/(y^{2})|= lim_(x → 0)(x(x + 2(-x)))/((-x)^{2}); = lim_(x → 0)\\frac{-x^{2}}{x^{2}}; =-1$.\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139534866 false] [17592186124176 1145 "Direct substitution gives an indeterminate form, but the function cannot be simplified by canceling a common factor in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\dfrac{0 \\cdot (0 + 2 \\cdot 0)}{0^2} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nLet's evaluate the limit approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2x)}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Taking $\\mathbf{x} \\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{x(x+2y)}{{y}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x+2(-x))}{(-x)^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the limit evaluates to different results on these two paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (0,0)}} \\dfrac{x(x + 2y)}{y^2} = \\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186124285 1100 "Find  $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}.$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186124285 1100 "Find $\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{x^2-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}}$." 13194139534866 false] [17592186124285 1145 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac00\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{2^2-4 \\cdot 1}{2-2\\sqrt{1}} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (2, 1))\\frac{x^{2} - 4y}{x - 2\\sqrt{y}} &= lim_(x → (2, 1))((x^{2} - 4y)(x + 2\\sqrt{y}))/((x - 2\\sqrt{y})(x + 2\\sqrt{y})) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (2, 1))((x^{2} - 4y)(x + 2\\sqrt{y}))/(x^{2} - (2\\sqrt{y})^{2}) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (2, 1))((x^{2} - 4y)(x + 2\\sqrt{y}))/(x^{2} - 4y) \\\\\n&= lim_(x → (2, 1))(x + 2\\sqrt{y})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → (2, 1))(x + 2\\sqrt{y}) &= 2 + 2\\sqrt{1} \\\\\n&= 2 + 2 \\\\\n&= 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4$." 13194139534866 false] [17592186124285 1145 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle{\\lim_{\\mathbf{x}\\to (2,1)}} \\dfrac{2^2-4 \\cdot 1}{2-2\\sqrt{1}} = \\dfrac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can simplify the limit by first multiplying the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-4y}{x-2\\sqrt{y}} & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{(x-2\\sqrt{y})(x+2\\sqrt{y})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-(2\\sqrt{y})^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}\\frac{({x}^{2}-4y)(x+2\\sqrt{y})}{{x}^{2}-4y} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that the limit has been simplified, we can evaluate it:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (2,1)}(x+2\\sqrt{y}) & =2+2\\sqrt{1} \\\\  & =2+2 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the limit evaluates to $4.$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186124370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315854 13194139534866 true] [17592186124370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315861 13194139534866 true] [17592186124370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315924 13194139534866 true] [17592186124370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315931 13194139534866 true] [17592186124370 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315938 13194139534866 true] [17592186124371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315868 13194139534866 true] [17592186124371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315875 13194139534866 true] [17592186124371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315917 13194139534866 true] [17592186124371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315945 13194139534866 true] [17592186124371 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315952 13194139534866 true] [17592186124375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315896 13194139534866 true] [17592186124375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315903 13194139534866 true] [17592186124375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315910 13194139534866 true] [17592186124375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315980 13194139534866 true] [17592186124479 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315882 13194139534866 true] [17592186124479 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315889 13194139534866 true] [17592186124479 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315959 13194139534866 true] [17592186124479 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315966 13194139534866 true] [17592186124479 1020 #edb/ref 17592186315973 13194139534866 true] [17592186124854 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534866 true] [17592186124854 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}\n$$\n\n- The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=x$\n- The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y=-x$\n- The limit exists and is equal to $1$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186124854 1100 "Which of the following statements are true regarding the limit given below?\n$lim_(x → (0, 0))\\frac{xy - xy^{2}}{x^{2} + 2xy}$\n\n1. The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y = x$\n2. The limit evaluates to $1$ along the path $y =-x$\n3. The limit exists and is equal to $1$" 13194139534866 false] [17592186124854 1145 "Attempting to evaluate the limit by direct substitution leads to the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}:$\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\frac{0\\cdot 0-0\\cdot {0}^{2}}{{0}^{2}+2\\cdot 0\\cdot 0}=\\frac{0}{0}\n$$\n\nHowever, the function cannot be simplified by canceling common factors in the numerator and denominator:\n\nWe attempt to evaluate the limit by approaching $(0,0)$ from different paths.\n\n- Statement I is false. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(x)-x(x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{3{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-x}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-0}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Taking $x\\to (0,0)$ along the path $y=-x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)\\,\\,\\,y=-x}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x(-x)-x(-x)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2x(-x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{-{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}}{-{x}^{2}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(1+x) \\\\  & =1+0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Because the limit evaluates to different results on two different paths, we conclude that the limit does not exist:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (0,0)}\\frac{xy-x{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+2xy}=\\text{DNE}\n$$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315854 1017 #edb/ref 17592186315855 13194139534866 true] [17592186315854 1089 "q-129322" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315854 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534866 true] [17592186315854 1100 "Find ${lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315854 1113 #uuid "990a5be3-4739-524e-8b7d-aa92f180e71a" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315854 1145 "Let the function $f(x,y)$ be defined as\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThe function $f(x,y)$ is continuous except when the denominator is zero. However, the point $(0,-1)$ does not cause the denominator to vanish, so we can calculate the limit by substituting $(0,-1)$ into the function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (0,-1)}\\frac{{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{{0}^{2}-(-1)^{2}}{{0}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{0+1} \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315855 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315856 13194139534866 true] [17592186315855 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315857 13194139534866 true] [17592186315855 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315858 13194139534866 true] [17592186315855 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315859 13194139534866 true] [17592186315855 1000 #edb/ref 17592186315860 13194139534866 true] [17592186315855 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534866 true] [17592186315855 1041 "selection" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315855 1104 #edb/ref 17592186315859 13194139534866 true] [17592186315855 1110 #uuid "634ef1b6-e346-5012-a77e-0377d6e5d91e" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315856 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315856 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534866 true] [17592186315856 1111 #uuid "4e613dc0-f2f7-58cc-b45c-b027c6f30f2b" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315857 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315857 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534866 true] [17592186315857 1111 #uuid "5e24b156-b156-5f3e-abeb-a2fcb2d2b2c8" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315858 1034 "4" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315858 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534866 true] [17592186315858 1111 #uuid "395251ae-c97f-53ba-b469-d086a8ef8de6" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315859 1034 "-1" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315859 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534866 true] [17592186315859 1111 #uuid "0c0e8ac9-634d-5d62-8a1e-7db4b9436ac2" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315860 1034 "-2" 13194139534866 true] [17592186315860 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534866 true] [17592186315860 111